[size=6][b]Hardcore Tactical Realism (HTR) — Combat & AI Overhaul for SpaceBourne 2[/b][/size]

On-foot combat in SpaceBourne 2 is an MMO bullet-sponge: a level 30 raider soaks up 15 000 HP plus 15 000 armor, your damage depends on weapon level, and headshots barely matter. [b]HTR replaces that with ballistics[/b]: what kills is the round, where it hits and what armor it hits. Enemies go down fast, fight smarter, bleed, break bones and throw grenades — and so can you. Fight from cover.

Inspired by Cyberpunk 2077 [i]Hardcore Realism[/i], Fallout: New Vegas [i]Realistic Combat Overhaul[/i] and [i]NPC AI Improvement[/i] (no code from those mods is used). Works together with my [url=https://github.com/hubertdungen/SpaceBourne2-HOTAS]SB2 HOTAS[/url] mod.

[b]Links:[/b] [url=https://github.com/hubertdungen/SpaceBourne2-HardcoreTacticalRealism]GitHub[/url] · [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/games/spacebourne2/mods/4]Nexus Mods[/url] · [url=https://steamcommunity.com/app/1646850/discussions/0/573805072991123580/]Steam discussion[/url] · [url=https://github.com/hubertdungen/SpaceBourne2-HOTAS]SB2 HOTAS[/url]. More realism mods for SpaceBourne 2 are in the works (engineering turrets, tactical first person, stealth, gear at any level, faction power, split ship/foot invert); they will be linked here as they come out.

[line]
[size=5][b]Install (2 minutes)[/b][/size]

HTR is a Lua mod for [b]UE4SS[/b] (the script loader most SpaceBourne 2 mods use). Pick one:

[b]A. One-click installer (recommended, includes UE4SS)[/b]
[list=1]
[*]Download HardcoreTacticalRealism-x.y.z.zip (the big one) from [url=https://github.com/hubertdungen/SpaceBourne2-HardcoreTacticalRealism/releases/latest]GitHub Releases[/url].
[*]Extract it anywhere (e.g. Downloads) and double-click [b]Install.cmd[/b].
    - Steam: found automatically.
    - GOG / other folder: drag your SpaceBourne 2 folder onto Install.cmd.
[*]Start the game. Done.
[/list]

[b]B. Lua-only file (Nexus, or -lite.zip on GitHub) — if you already have UE4SS[/b] (for example from SB2 HOTAS):
[list=1]
[*]Open your game folder (...\SpaceBourne 2, the one with SpaceBourne2.exe).
[*]Extract the zip [b]there[/b]. It creates SB2\Binaries\Win64\Mods\HardcoreTacticalRealism and switches itself on — nothing else to edit.
[*]No UE4SS yet? Get [url=https://github.com/UE4SS-RE/RE-UE4SS/releases/tag/v3.0.1]RE-UE4SS 3.0.1[/url] and copy dwmapi.dll, UE4SS.dll and UE4SS-settings.ini into SpaceBourne 2\SB2\Binaries\Win64\ — or simply use option A.
[/list]

[b]Update:[/b] install the new version the same way (the installer keeps your config.lua).
[b]Uninstall:[/b] Uninstall.cmd (option A) or delete the HardcoreTacticalRealism folder (option B). HTR never writes to your saves, so you can add or remove it at any time.

[line]
[size=5][b]Controls[/b][/size]

[code]
Key                     What it does
F7                      Next mode: Tactical → Tactical Hardcore → Realism → Realism Hardcore (shown on the HUD)
Shift+F7                Gear bonus on/off (off by default; see below). Was F8 before 0.4.3, which is also the game's quick load
F9                      Ships vs infantry on/off (shown on the HUD)
F11                     Slow perks on/off (on by default; see below)
Gamepad (experimental)  press Switch camera 4 times quickly = next mode; crouched = ships toggle
[/code]

All choices are remembered between sessions. Keys can be changed in config.lua.

[line]
[size=5][b]Perks (F11 — slow by default)[/b][/size]

With realistic damage, perks such as the turret, barricade, ground slam, shield or slow time turn into super powers. HTR makes the suit's perk energy refill [b]4x slower on foot[/b] and the turret / barricade / ground-slam / auto-revive cooldowns run 4x slower, so a perk is a tactical tool you save for the right moment. Ship energy is not affected. F11 switches back to vanilla.

[line]
[size=5][b]Modes[/b][/size]

[code]
Mode                Damage you and your allies take                             Wounds on your side
Tactical (default)  follows weapon and hit zone, always 100–150 % of vanilla    rare
Tactical Hardcore   Tactical, then all damage x2 (both sides)                   occasional
Realism             exactly the same rules as enemies; your suit is your armor  full
Realism Hardcore    Realism, then all damage x2 (both sides)                    full, bleeding x2
[/code]

Enemies always use the realistic model.

[line]
[size=5][b]Damage model[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Damage = [b]caliber vs armor + hit zone[/b]. NPC level, weapon level and rarity no longer matter (unless you switch on the small optional gear bonus, Shift+F7).
[*]Unarmored targets drop in [b]1–2 rifle hits[/b], heavy armor in [b]5–6[/b], power armor and robots in [b]6–8[/b]. Snipers kill almost anything in one hit; melee kills in one or two blows.
[*][b]Headshots[/b] kill a bare head with any firearm, and [b]no helmet stops a headshot completely[/b] (at least 40 % always gets through): 1–2 headshots kill through any helmet. This applies to everyone — NPCs shooting NPCs included (vanilla has no NPC headshots).
[*]Armor [b]wears down[/b] as it stops rounds (the armor bar shows its condition); worn armor protects less. Vanilla's armor-overflow bug is gone.
[*]Destroyed limbs use the game's own dismemberment; lethal hits blow off the part that was hit (needs [i]Gore[/i] on in Gameplay settings).
[/list]

[code]
Hits to kill (centre mass / head)  none   light  medium     heavy      power
Pistol                             2 / 1  4 / 1  6 / 2      7 / 2      8 / 2
SMG                                2 / 1  4 / 1  6 / 2      8 / 2      8 / 2
Rifle                              2 / 1  3 / 1  4 / 1      5 / 2      6 / 2
Hunter (DMR)                       2 / 1  2 / 1  2 / 1      3 / 1      3 / 1
Sniper                             1 / 1  1 / 1  1 / 1      1 / 1      2 / 1
Knife / Sword / Hammer             1      1      2 / 1 / 1  2 / 2 / 1  3 / 2 / 2
[/code]

Shotgun pellets count individually (one close blast kills an unarmored target). Bosses have a tougher body (about 8–9 rifle hits, 3 to the head). Ground turrets (team battles, bases) are heavy emplaced guns: one round every 1.5 s, one hit kills an unarmored target, two kill through any armor.

[line]
[size=5][b]Gear bonus (optional, Shift+F7 — off by default)[/b][/size]

For players who still like a bit of RPG feel. With the gear bonus on, weapon and armor [b]level[/b] (and item rarity) add a [b]small extra[/b] on top of the ballistics — they never decide whether a weapon works. A level 5 rifle still drops a level 60 raider in a few hits; a level 60 rifle just needs one hit fewer.

[list]
[*][b]Weapon:[/b] up to +8 penetration points and +15 % damage.
[*][b]Armor:[/b] up to +8 protection points and 25 % less wear (only where there is armor).
[*][b]Scale:[/b] diminishing returns — level 1 gives nothing, level 10 gives 56 % of the bonus, level 30 gives 83 %, level 60 the full bonus. Each rarity step above common counts as 5 levels.
[*][b]Whose level:[/b] your weapon item and your level for your suit; NPCs and turrets their own level; squad soldiers your level. It works the same for both sides, so it also fits the Realism modes (you can switch it off per mode in config.lua). In the Tactical modes the damage you and your allies take stays at 100–150 % of vanilla, so there the bonus mostly changes the damage your side deals.
[/list]

[code]
Rifle vs heavy armor, hits to kill  armor L1  armor L10  armor L30  armor L60
Rifle L1                            5         6          6          6
Rifle L10                           4         5          5          5
Rifle L30                           4         4          5          5
Rifle L60                           4         4          4          5
[/code]

Gear bonus off (the default) = the tables above, for every level.

[line]
[size=5][b]Wounds and healing[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Bleeding:[/b] a round that gets through the armor can open a wound that drains HP for 15–25 s until the suit's nanites clot it (up to 3 wounds at once). Bleeding stops healing.
[*][b]Broken bones:[/b] a heavy hit to an arm or leg can break it. NPCs with a broken arm aim badly; for you, healing is halved until the nanites fuse the bone (about a minute).
[*][b]Robots[/b] never bleed, but a heavy hit can still wreck an arm (shaky aim) or a leg.
[*][b]Squad soldiers[/b] bleed too; once they go down, the game's own bleed-out timer takes over and a medic (or you) can still bring them back.
[*]Enemies get wounded in every mode. You and your allies get wounded rarely in Tactical, more in Tactical Hardcore, and fully in Realism; Hardcore modes bleed twice as fast.
[*][b]Healing:[/b] your suit's own healing is kept but slowed by mode — Tactical 70 %, Tactical Hardcore 50 %, Realism 25 %, Realism Hardcore 12 % of vanilla — and it starts 8–15 s after your last hit. Armor nanites slowly repair the suit too.
[/list]

[line]
[size=5][b]Explosions, grenades and ships[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Explosions[/b] (your grenades, NPC grenades, rockets) do realistic damage that fades with distance and gibs at close range. In vanilla, NPCs ignore every explosion that isn't yours.
[*][b]NPC grenades:[/b] regular and elite troops throw grenades when you hide behind cover too long.
[*][b]Ships vs infantry (F9):[/b] ship guns — yours and AI ships' — now hit people on foot. A direct hit kills almost anyone and the blast hurts everyone nearby, friends included.
[/list]

[line]
[size=5][b]Friendly fire[/b][/size]

[code]
Attacker → victim            Tactical modes                Realism modes
You → companions / squad     35 %                          100 %
Companions / squad → you     35 %                          100 %
Ally → ally, allied turrets  vanilla, max 10 % HP per hit  same
Enemy → enemy, ally ↔ enemy  100 % (realistic)             same
[/code]

Ground turrets fight for their own team. Your squad soldiers now take realistic damage too: in the Tactical modes they go down injured first (as vanilla); in the Realism modes headshots and heavy overkill kill outright and other lethal hits almost always do (80 % / 95 %), so both sides follow the same lethal rules. They wear at least medium armor and a helmet, so they can hold a firefight instead of dropping as soon as they show up.

[line]
[size=5][b]AI and doctrines[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]NPC bullets fly at 360–500 m/s instead of 100 m/s, but NPCs aim like people: spread by training, a settle-in time after spotting you, worse aim while moving, hurt or suppressed, and centre-mass aim instead of vanilla's random feet/above-the-head points.
[*]Vanilla behaviour that never triggered now works: breaking line of sight when hit, falling back when badly hurt, calling allies, peeking from cover every 2.5 s instead of 5 s.
[*][b]NPCs keep fighting:[/b] an NPC that dives for cover fights from there for a few seconds before your fire can send it running again (no more enemies that only run under fire), and it resumes firing as soon as it aims at you again.
[*][b]Your squad keeps up:[/b] vanilla squad soldiers walk, fall behind and then teleport next to you. HTR makes them run and sprint after you, so only a stuck soldier still teleports.
[*][b]Suppression:[/b] your rounds landing close make enemies duck and shoot worse.
[*][b]Situational awareness:[/b] relaxed enemies (no fight yet) only notice you up close; once alert (they spotted you, got hit or were warned) they spot and shoot much farther.
[*][b]Arrival grace:[/b] after an elevator ride, a new dungeon floor or a level load, and for freshly spawned NPCs, everyone (enemies and allies) aims very badly for 3-4 s, so nobody gets massacred the moment a floor loads.
[*]Companions' bullets work (vanilla gave them a 0-second projectile life).
[/list]

[code]
Doctrine   Who                               How they fight
Pirate     raiders, salvagers                slow, spray, wide spread, may panic when mates die or flee when badly hurt
Militia    low-tier troops                   better than pirates, still sloppy
Regular    standard faction troops           quick, accurate, cover, grenades
Elite      high-tier troops, guards, bosses  fastest reactions, tight aim, grenades, alert the area
Machine    robots                            fearless, steady, never retreat
Companion  your allies                       elite-level aim, no panic
[/code]

[line]
[size=5][b]Settings[/b][/size]

SB2\Binaries\Win64\Mods\HardcoreTacticalRealism\scripts\config.lua (open with Notepad, restart the game after saving): mode and keys, every feature on/off, Hardcore multiplier, helmet rule, wounds and healing per mode, gear bonus strength, ships vs infantry strength, perk speed, enemy awareness ranges, squad pace, friendly fire, boss toughness, logging. Deeper tuning: htr\ballistics.lua (calibers, armor, hit zones) and htr\doctrines.lua (how each kind of NPC fights).

[line]
[size=5][b]Known limitations[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]On-foot combat only; ship-vs-ship combat is unchanged.
[*]The game has no faction field on soldiers, so doctrines use its raider flag, troop tier, guard/robot/boss flags and companion status.
[*]Gamepad toggles are experimental (untested without a controller).
[*]Tested on the Steam build of 26 Oct 2026. Bug reports with htr.log (set Debug.Log = true) are very welcome; if you ever notice stutter, htr.log names any HTR code that took longer than 8 ms.
[*][b]Versions before 0.3.2 froze the game for a moment on every shot[/b] — fixed in 0.3.2; please update. In-game tests while firing now show the same frame times as the game without mods.
[/list]

[line]
[size=5][b]After a game update[/b][/size]

Every game-internal name lives in one file, htr\gamedefs.lua. If an update renames something, HTR switches that feature off (logged in UE4SS.log) instead of crashing. tools\ holds the scripts used to inspect the game's Blueprints.

